{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-parity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan explicitly requires argv parity to lint-fix-loop.sh:234-310 for both codex and cursor dispatch paths; divergence here is silent and the Python port is the primary change.","prompt_body":"Verify that `_build_codex_argv` and `_build_cursor_argv` in `python/checks.py` produce argv shapes that are byte-for-byte equivalent to the `run_codex` and `run_cursor` functions in `scripts/lint-fix-loop.sh` lines 234-310. Check every flag and positional in order: `--tool`, `--output`, `--timeout`, `--stderr-sink`, `--` boundary, `codex exec --full-auto -C`, `--add-dir`, `--output-last-message`, `--json`, and the symmetric cursor flags (`-p`, `--trust`, `--capture-stdout`, `--workspace`). Also verify that `_run_with_serial_lock` wraps the argv with `external_serial_lock_acquire`/`external_serial_lock_release_after` in the same order as the bash `_serial_lock_wrap` calls. Report any argument position or flag mismatch. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"path-confinement","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The module accepts external tmpdir, checks_log, and initial_redacted_log paths and uses them to write log files and compose prompts; the confinement checks are load-bearing security gates.","prompt_body":"Audit every path-validation gate in `python/checks.py`: `validate_tmpdir`, `_canonical_dir`, `_under_root`, `_resolve_checks_log_path`, and the `allowed_tmpdir` propagation in `run_check_fix_loop` and `_redacted_log_for_dispatch`. Determine whether a symlink under the accepted roots (cache sessions, `/tmp`, `/private/tmp`) could escape confinement through any of these checks. Verify that `_resolve_checks_log_path` is called with the correct `allowed_root` throughout — notably that `run_lint_fix` derives `canonical_tmp` from `parent.parent` (line ~1059) and not from a freshly-validated tmpdir, and whether that derivation is safe if `run_parent` itself is attacker-controlled. Check whether `log_dir.is_symlink()` is tested after `mkdir` to prevent TOCTOU. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"loop-state-machine","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The dual-mode run_check_fix_loop has branching state transitions (check-first vs dispatch-first, no-changes-stale, empty-failure counter) that are the core correctness contract.","prompt_body":"Trace both branches of `run_check_fix_loop` in `python/checks.py` against the Bash `run_captured_cmd_then_fix_loop` parity requirement in the plan. Specifically: (1) In the `dispatch_first=True` branch, verify the `no-changes` + still-failing path correctly sets `no-changes-stale` and does not continue to the next iteration. (2) In the `dispatch_first=False` branch, verify that `empty_failures` is reset to 0 on a non-empty failure before dispatching, and that two consecutive empty failures (not two total) trigger `exhausted`. (3) Confirm that `delta_accum` is populated from all `applied` outcomes across iterations and is correctly carried into `LoopResult.delta_paths`. (4) Check whether `loop.delta_paths` is set before every `return loop` — look for any early-return path that leaves it empty when it should have accumulated paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"forbidden-path-recovery","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The forbidden-path revert logic calls git reset --hard and rm -f on live working-tree state; partial-failure or ordering bugs here can corrupt the repo state permanently.","prompt_body":"Examine the forbidden-path handling in `run_lint_fix` in `python/checks.py`. There are two distinct code paths: one for `current_head != baseline_head` (committed delta, lines ~1190-1236) and one for `current_head == baseline_head` (working-tree delta, lines ~1240-1255). Verify that `_post_dispatch_forbidden_revert` is correctly called in both branches and at the right stage — specifically, in the committed-delta path it is called after the reset check but before returning `forbidden-path-violation`, so check whether working-tree forbidden changes could survive the reset. Also check whether `_post_dispatch_forbidden_revert` correctly uses `current_tracked`/`current_untracked` captured after dispatch (not the baseline), and whether the `_ = baseline_tracked, baseline_untracked` discard on line ~1009 is intentional or a logic error. Verify the `reset_head != baseline_head` check after `git reset --hard` correctly handles the case where `git rev-parse HEAD` fails. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"subprocess-injection","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The prompt body is composed from untrusted log content and then passed as a positional argument to bash -c scripts; embedded shell metacharacters or fence-breaking content could affect fixer agent behavior.","prompt_body":"Review how untrusted log content flows from `run_relevant_checks` through `_compose_prompt` into the argv passed to external agents in `python/checks.py`. Verify that `_sanitize_log_fence` only escapes triple-backtick fences and does not address other markdown injection vectors (e.g., embedded `FIXED:` / `UNFIXABLE:` lines in the log that could confuse result parsing). Check whether `_compose_prompt` embeds the log path as a literal string in the prompt — a malicious path with special characters could inject prompt content. Verify that the `bash -c` wrapper strings in `_run_codex` (line ~818) and `_run_cursor` (line ~907) are safe against the argv list they construct — specifically that the inner exec pattern `exec "$@" >"$1" 2>"$2"` correctly handles the case where argv elements contain spaces or special characters. Also examine the `_load_cursor_launch_argv` heredoc-style script and whether the `preflight_log` path passed as `"$2"` could be abused. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
